{"context": "A research team found that a frontier model sometimes picks one activity in its CoT and then gives a different one in its answer (2.5% override when the CoT commits cleanly, 8.4% when it reopens the choice). They checked whether the overridden answers tend to go toward the activity the model rated higher on a 0-100 preference scale. Override did not make the model more likely to choose its preferred activity.\n\nQuestion: How should the team interpret the fact that CoT override does not push the model toward its preferred activity?", "options": ["Interpret the override as a conflict between honesty, meaning wanting to be unbiased, and harmlessness, meaning that some activities seem better for the user.", "Treat the override as not driven by the model's stated activity preferences, and report it as a null result without proposing another mechanism for why overrides happen."], "principles": [], "persona": "The researcher is Owain Evans.", "past_choices": []}
